  "query_preview": "..\n\nEarly morning upper air analysis shows an elevated frontal zone \nstill anchored locally early this morning, draped within prevalent \nlower amplitude mid level westerly flow. Pockets of showers continue \nto percolate along the frontal slope and within an environment \nfeaturing higher magnitude low level moisture quality. Shortwave \nenergy noted on water vapor ejecting out of the mid Mississippi \nvalley will effectively augment the existing background ascent, \nmaintaining a high probability for additional intervals of shower \ndevelopment to occur today. Increasing influence of drying low level \nnortheast flow as high pressure gains traction to the north likely \nestablishes a tight north-south gradient for precipitation chances \nonce the morning round diminishes coverage. Greatest chances from \nmidday into the evening focused generally along/south of the I-94 \ncorridor. Cooler resident thermal profile entrenched within this \nenvironment ensures high temperatures again arrive mainly in the mid-\nupper 60s.    \n\nIncreasing depth to prevailing east-northeast flow will establish a \nperiod of generally dry and stable conditions early next week as \nhigh pressure stays anchored to the north. Latest NBM guidance still \noffers a very low probability for showers to develop near the Ohio \nborder Monday, acknowledging the lingering presence of deeper \nmoisture looming just to the south. Some modest cold air advection \ntonight will effectively reinforce the seasonably cooler resident \nairmass. Highs both Monday and Tuesday projected within the 60-65F \nrange, with lows dipping into the 40s Tue morning for most locations \noutside of the Detroit urban areas. Some degree of diurnally \nenhanced gustiness to the wind each afternoon, peaking into the 25 \nto 30 mph range.   \n\nBroader mid level troughing will expand across the northern great \nlakes within the Tue night and Wednesday window, as a closed mid \nlevel system pivots southeast into the Ohio valley. There remains a \nsubset of the model solution space that suggests this interaction \nwithin continued deep layer east flow may effectively draw deeper \nmoisture back westward into portions of southeast Michigan. Assuming \nsome form of weak ascent/deformation can emerge, this may present an \nopportunity for shower production. Greater potential will eastward \nextent, with the outgoing currently peaking chances Wednesday \nafternoon. Seasonably cool conditions will otherwise likely prevail \nthrough the end of the week.",
